From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 17:43:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 07/77] Generic routines keep their unit's access to private
 members (#40670)

Since #40621 a private member of a generic record or class is checked
against the generic's unit, but a global generic routine specialized in
another unit is checked against the specializing unit, so a generic
routine can no longer access private members of a type declared in its
own unit. Treat code of a specialized routine (or a routine nested in
one) as belonging to the unit that declares the generic routine.
---
 compiler/symtable.pas | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/compiler/symtable.pas b/compiler/symtable.pas
index ccac2138..5d5d7bc7 100644
--- a/compiler/symtable.pas
+++ b/compiler/symtable.pas
@@ -3318,6 +3318,8 @@ implementation
         curstruct : tabstractrecorddef;
 
       function is_current_unit(st:tsymtable):boolean;
+        var
+          pi : tprocinfo;
         begin
           result :=
             (
@@ -3330,6 +3332,20 @@ implementation
                 st.iscurrentunit
               )
             );
+          { a generic routine that is specialized in another unit keeps the
+            access rights of the unit that declares it (#40670) }
+          if not result and not assigned(curstruct) then
+            begin
+              pi:=current_procinfo;
+              while assigned(pi) do
+                begin
+                  if pi.procdef.is_specialization and
+                     assigned(pi.procdef.genericdef) and
+                     (pi.procdef.genericdef.owner.moduleid=st.moduleid) then
+                    exit(true);
+                  pi:=pi.parent;
+                end;
+            end;
         end;
 
       var
